'''birch-camphor''' (bėrch′kam″fọr), ''n.'' A resinous substance obtained from the bark of the black birch.

'''birchen''' (bėr′chẹn), ''a.'' [= Sc. ''birken, birkin,'' < ME. ''birchen, birkin,'' < AS. *''bircen'' (Somner) (= D. LG. ''berken'' = OHG. ''birchin,'' MHG. G. ''birken''), < ''birce,'' birch: see ''birch.''] Of or pertaining to birch; consisting or made of birch: as, “''birchen'' brooms,” ''Beau. and Fl.,'' Loyal Subject.
{{smaller block|We say of a wanton child, . . . he must be annoynted with ''byrchin'' salve.
{{right|''Tyndale,'' Works (1573), p. 166. (''N. E. D.'')}}
His beaver’d brow a ''birchen'' garland wears.
{{right|''Pope,'' Dunciad, iv. 141.}}}}

'''birch-oil''' (bėrch′oil), ''n.'' An oil extracted from birch-bark, said to be used in preparing Russia leather.

'''birch-water''' (bėrch′wâ″tėr), ''n.'' The sap of the birch. See ''birch-wine.''

'''birch-wine''' (bėrch′wīn), ''n.'' A fermented liquor made from the sap of the birch-tree, which is collected in the spring throughout the mountainous and wooded districts of Germany and Scandinavia. {{smaller|It is called by names which signify birch-water or birch-wine in the different languages. It is said}}

